      "id": "ministry",
      "title": "Ministry of Jesus",
      "note": "From John's preaching to the week of the Passion, ordered by what the Gospels say came before what rather than dated. Mark's order is the backbone of the Galilean ministry, John's feasts and notes of time frame the whole, and Luke's order places what he alone tells. Where the Gospels part ways — Mark and Luke on when the fishermen were called, Matthew's gathering of the miracles in chapters 8 and 9 — one order is kept and the other left out. A. T. Robertson's *Harmony of the Gospels* (1922) is a further witness: where its order agrees with the Gospels' an ordering rests on both, and where it orders what they leave open it rests on Robertson alone. Two of its placements are not taken: John's beheading, which Robertson tells where Herod hears of Jesus (Mark tells it looking back), and the anointing at Bethany, which Robertson sets two days before the Passover with Mark and Matthew where John has it six days before. Events no line joins are of unknown order to one another.",
